From: WFI-040 — widen the shell write-detection vocabulary
Subject: [PATCH] fix(guard): recognise the writers outside the five-verb vocabulary

Human-applied patch. guard-invariants.json is a protected gate artifact.
See docs/workflow-improvements/WFI-040.md.

Apply with:
    git apply reports/notes/wfi-040b-shell-write-vocabulary.patch
    python3 plugins/sdd-quality-loop/scripts/generate-guard-invariants.py

Regeneration is REQUIRED: the guard reads the four generated modules, not this
JSON. Refreshing the installed plugin under ~/.claude/plugins/cache/ is required
before the change is live on a developer machine.

WHY THIS IS DATA-ONLY. The decision path is three gates
(sdd-hook-guard.py:1423-1433): the command must reference a protected path, then
SHELL_SUDO_WRITE_RE must match, and only then is _shell_write_targets_are_safe
consulted. The measured 15/15 gap was not one missing check but two missing
entries -- the writers appear in neither the regex nor the vocabulary, so the
analysis was never reached. Adding them to `indirect_cmds` makes
_shell_write_targets_are_safe fail closed on them, and extending
`sudo_write_re` makes the second gate admit them at all. No guard logic changes,
so the Python, JS and PowerShell twins stay behaviourally identical by
construction.

WHY indirect RATHER THAN write_arg/write_dest. `install src dst` and `ln -sf src
dst` could be judged by destination, but `dd if=s of=d` names its target in an
`of=` token that the argument scanner does not model, and `patch` and `git
apply` name theirs inside a file the analyser never opens. Judging some
precisely and others not would leave the vocabulary looking complete while
staying selectively blind. Fail-closed is uniform and honest.

KNOWN FALSE-POSITIVE COST, stated rather than discovered later. A command that
references a protected path AND contains one of these verbs is now denied even
when it only reads -- for example `python3 -c` printing a protected file. That
is the WFI-022 direction, and it is a real cost. It is bounded by the first
gate: commands that never name a protected path are unaffected, which is the
overwhelming majority. `sed` is admitted only in its `-i` form and `git` only on
worktree-writing subcommands, so `sed -n` and `git log` stay reads.

NOT CLOSED BY THIS PATCH. `git apply x.patch` and `patch -p1 -i x.patch` name
their targets inside the patch file, so they never reference a protected path on
the command line and stop at the first gate. Closing those needs a code change
at sdd-hook-guard.py:1423 in all three twins, tracked separately.

--- a/plugins/sdd-quality-loop/references/guard-invariants.json
+++ b/plugins/sdd-quality-loop/references/guard-invariants.json
@@ -138,10 +138,22 @@
     "indirect_cmds": [
       "eval",
       "xargs",
       "source",
       "sh",
       "bash",
       "zsh",
       "dash",
-      "ksh"
+      "ksh",
+      "sed",
+      "patch",
+      "install",
+      "ln",
+      "truncate",
+      "dd",
+      "python",
+      "python3",
+      "perl",
+      "ruby",
+      "node",
+      "git"
     ],
@@ -163,6 +175,6 @@
     "cd_cmds": [
       "cd",
       "pushd"
     ],
-    "sudo_write_re": "(?:>|>>|\\btee\\b|\\btouch\\b|\\bcp\\b|\\bmv\\b|\\brm\\b|\\bSet-Content\\b|\\bOut-File\\b|\\bNew-Item\\b|\\bRemove-Item\\b)",
+    "sudo_write_re": "(?:>|>>|\\btee\\b|\\btouch\\b|\\bcp\\b|\\bmv\\b|\\brm\\b|\\bSet-Content\\b|\\bOut-File\\b|\\bNew-Item\\b|\\bRemove-Item\\b|\\bpatch\\b|\\binstall\\b|\\bln\\b|\\btruncate\\b|\\bdd\\b|\\bpython3?\\b|\\bperl\\b|\\bruby\\b|\\bnode\\b|\\bsed\\b[^|;&]*\\s-i\\b|\\bgit\\s+(?:apply|checkout|restore|stash|reset|clean|mv|rm|merge|rebase|pull)\\b)",
     "read_only_start_re": "^\\s*(?:cat|ls|test|grep|stat|head|tail|rg)\\b"
